.TH CLAUDE-BARDO 1 2026-09-30 "claude-bardo 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
claude-bardo \- bring Claude Code sessions back after a crash or a reboot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude-bardo
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claude-bardo
remembers every interactive Claude Code session through its SessionStart and
SessionEnd hooks, and relaunches the ones that died: each in its own terminal
window, in its own directory, with its own flags, mid-conversation.
.PP
Nothing needs to be run before a reboot: the hooks keep the registry current.
A terminal crash (SIGHUP), a reboot or a macOS logout (SIGTERM) ends a session with reason
.BR other ;
a SIGKILL or a power cut leaves no end at all but a lingering
.IR ~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json .
Both are restorable.
.BR /exit ,
.BR /clear ,
.B /resume
and Claude Code's own
.B /logout
are deliberate: those sessions never come back.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR list " [" \-\-all "] [" \-\-json ]
What died and what is live. The default command.
.TP
.BR restore " [" \fIid\fR ...] [" \-\-all "] [" \-\-older "] [" \-\-tmux "] [" \-\-terminal " \fIapp\fR] [" \-n ]
Relaunch restorable sessions. Without ids, a picker opens with the latest
death wave (everything that died within 30 minutes of the newest death)
pre-selected and older deaths below it.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the commands instead.
.TP
.B restore \-\-fork
Open a copy of each live session (new id, same history) in the chosen
terminal. The originals keep running: a rehearsal of a restore, or a way to
move sessions to another terminal app.
.TP
.BI resume " id"
Relaunch one session in the current terminal.
.TP
.BR forget " [\fIid\fR ...] [" \-\-forks ]
Mark sessions as closed on purpose: they leave the picker.
.B \-\-forks
closes every dead copy that
.B restore \-\-fork
made.
.TP
.BR prune " [" \-\-days " \fIn\fR]"
Drop closed and unresumable history older than
.I n
days (default 30).
.TP
.B doctor
Check the hooks, the state directory, the terminal and the Claude Code
internals this tool relies on.
.TP
.BR install " [" \-\-launchd "] [" \-n ]
Copy the binary to
.IR ~/.local/bin ,
add the hooks to
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
(with a backup, never touching other hooks) and register running sessions.
.B \-\-launchd
adds a login agent that opens the picker when the last shutdown killed
something.
.TP
.BR uninstall " [" \-\-purge ]
Remove the hooks, the login agent and the binary;
.B \-\-purge
also deletes the registry.
.PP
Id prefixes need at least 4 characters and must match one session.
.SH REPLAY
Known flags and their values are replayed from the process's exact argv.
Never replayed: the prompt, session selectors
.RB ( \-\-resume ,
.BR \-\-continue ,
.BR \-\-session\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ),
one-shot modes
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-\-bg )
and
.BR \-\-worktree .
Permission bypass
.RB ( \-\-dangerously\-skip\-permissions ,
.BR "\-\-permission\-mode bypassPermissions" ,
and friends) is dropped unless
.B restore \-\-keep\-bypass
is given; the login agent never keeps it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_BARDO_DIR
State directory (default
.IR ~/.local/state/claude-bardo ).
.TP
.B CLAUDE_BARDO_TERMINAL
.BR ghostty " (default), " iterm ", " terminal ", " alacritty ", " wezterm " or " kitty .
.TP
.B CLAUDE_BARDO_LAUNCHER
A command template for any other terminal, with a
.B {cmd}
token, e.g.
.BR "myterm \-e {cmd}" .
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Claude Code's directory (default
.IR ~/.claude ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/claude-bardo/registry.jsonl
One JSON event per session start and end (schema
.BR v:1 ),
mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/claude-bardo/bardo.log
Hook errors and relaunch failures.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 ok, 1 failure, 2 usage error, 3 nothing to restore or nothing selected.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
claude-bardo                              # what died, what is live
claude-bardo restore                      # after a reboot: Enter
claude-bardo restore \-\-fork \-\-terminal iterm   # rehearse, originals untouched
claude-bardo list \-\-json | jq .status
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR fzf (1)
